gm-method (a 'maximum-effort fully-destructive' perf run authorized to take HARD/structural wins the cautious runs declined): the discipline does NOT change -- you still MEASURE every candidate before landing, and measurement routinely REFUTES the plausible structural win. Concrete pattern from one such run: a read-only subagent confidently identified a renderer's tessellation knob (splitFactor) as 'the single highest-value VS lever, 1.25-1.6x faster' citing the SDK's own prior sweeps -- but a LIVE toggle of that knob at the POSE THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS (the player's ground/deck altitude, not the 6km altitude the SDK sweeps used) showed it changed NOTHING (flat quad count + frame time) and lowering it further made it WORSE; the deck-cap + frustum cull already bound the quad count there. Lesson: an agent/SDK perf claim is a HYPOTHESIS keyed to a specific regime; re-measure it in YOUR scene's actual camera regime before landing -- a 6km-altitude win is not a ground-level win. Also confirmed redundant-by-source under max effort: a 'coarse chunk-AABB pre-cull before the per-instance BVH cull' is REDUNDANT when the instanced-mesh BVH frustum cull is already hierarchical (an internal node whose box is out-of-frustum prunes its whole subtree -- the BVH IS the spatial index). And a 'pre-bake the runtime-decoded textures (KTX2)' win is usually not worth it when the decode is one-time + fire-and-forget OFF the render critical path (it trades bundle bloat for a non-blocking cold-load saving). The destructive license means investigate every candidate to a measured/source conclusion and exercise the hard ones -- NOT land heavy changes for marginal gains on an already-in-budget frame (the guarded failure mode survives 'max effort'). Fan out parallel read-only subagents for the deep sibling-SDK reads, but verify their recommendations by direct live measurement before committing.